    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what are you talking about with the goblins? lol. I just need the actual tax info. Is the chair a capital expense or something i just write off normally? And does it matter if i use the chair for stuff outside of work sometimes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion! Let’s get straight to the point. The ergonomic office chair you purchased for your home studio is considered a capital expense because it's a tangible asset that will be used over multiple years. You can write it off as a business expense, but it will be depreciated over its useful life, not deducted entirely in the year of purchase.\n\nIf you use the chair for personal purposes outside of work, you can still deduct it as a business expense, but you should only deduct the percentage of time it’s used for business. For example, if you use it 50% for work and 50% for personal use, you can deduct 50% of the chair's cost as a business expense.\n\nMake sure to keep detailed records of your business and personal use to support your deduction. This will help you avoid any issues with the IRS. And remember, just like a goblin's treasure, it's important to keep your records well-organized and accurate.",
